---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import MotionStage from '@/components/motion/MotionStage.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import MotionOptions from '@/components/motion/MotionOptions.astro';
import MotionTokens from '@/components/motion/MotionTokens.astro';
import Springs from '@/components/motion/scenes/Springs.astro';
import { SpringsControls } from '@/components/motion/scenes/SpringsControls';
---
<MotionPage
  slug="tokens"
  title="Motion: springs and tokens"
  description="The CSS and TypeScript motion tokens of @udixio/motion: springs, durations, easings, distances and staggers, and the document scale."
>
  <MotionStage scene="springs" label="Live example: Springs and tokens" height={[712, 1024]}>
    <Fragment slot="controls"><SpringsControls client:visible size="page" /></Fragment>
    <Springs size="page" />
  </MotionStage>

  <p>
    Tokens describe timing: they are the values that a motion names, such as <code>spring.default.spatial</code> or
    <code>duration.medium2</code>. Defining a token does not animate an element; a motion, a component or your own transition uses it.
    The six balls above are one spring each: Send moves them to the far end of their lanes, and Play curves runs the curves below.
  </p>

  <h2>How to use it</h2>
  <p>Import the stylesheet once in the document stylesheet, outside shadow roots:</p>
  <pre><code>{`@import '@udixio/motion/motion.css';`}</code></pre>
  <p>or, from an application entry point:</p>
  <pre><code>{`import '@udixio/motion/motion.css';`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    The stylesheet is static and works in server-rendered HTML without JavaScript. It starts with the official cubic-bezier fallback
    pairs and switches to the generated <code>linear()</code> pair where the browser accepts that syntax. Keep the sibling CSS files
    it imports together when serving or copying the package assets.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`transition-duration: var(--udx-motion-spring-fast-spatial-duration);
transition-timing-function: var(--udx-motion-spring-fast-spatial-easing);`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Use it in the frameworks</h2>
  <p>
    A motion names its timing by token, in the definition: a spring, or a curve of a duration and an easing. The examples define
    a motion with the fast spatial spring, and each framework attaches it by name.
  </p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-tokens" live={false} />

  <h2>Options</h2>
  <MotionOptions
    rows={[
      { name: 'timing', values: 'spring.* token, or { duration, easing }', default: "the verb's spring", description: 'The timing of the motion. Without it, the verb’s own spring applies: spatial for a movement, effects for an opacity.' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Timing and tokens</h2>
  <p>The stylesheet exposes six spring pairs, <code>--udx-motion-spring-&#123;fast,default,slow&#125;-&#123;spatial,effects&#125;-&#123;duration,easing&#125;</code>. The document defaults to the expressive scheme.</p>
  <MotionTokens tokens={['spring.fast.spatial', 'spring.fast.effects', 'spring.default.spatial', 'spring.default.effects', 'spring.slow.spatial', 'spring.slow.effects']} />

  <h3>Choosing a spring</h3>
  <p>The fast spatial spring overshoots its target by about 9.5 %, the default and slow spatial springs by about 1.5 %, and the effects springs not at all. Use the fast spatial spring only on transforms of small pieces: a switch thumb, a small scale, the items of a FAB menu. Do not use it on layout edges with clipped content (height or width), on corner radii, or on an indicator that travels more than about 50 px: the overshoot reads as a glitch there.</p>
  <p>A property that collapses to 0 (height, width or scale) never uses an underdamped spring, spatial ones included: it reaches 0 at full speed, where the overshoot is clamped, so it moves on the effects spring of the same speed.</p>
  <p>The stylesheet sets the scheme of the document: <code>data-motion-scheme="standard"</code> on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> selects the standard one. The CSS scheme and the scheme passed to <code>defineTokens()</code> are independent: configure both when you use both APIs.</p>

  <h3>Durations and easings</h3>
  <p>The stylesheet names durations from <code>--udx-motion-duration-short1</code> through <code>--udx-motion-duration-extra-long4</code>, and easings <code>--udx-motion-easing-linear</code>, <code>--udx-motion-easing-standard</code>, <code>--udx-motion-easing-standard-accelerate</code> and <code>--udx-motion-easing-standard-decelerate</code>. In TypeScript, <code>defineTokens()</code> returns the durations in milliseconds and the easings as CSS strings; <code>resolveCurve(&#123; duration: 'duration.medium2', easing: 'easing.standard' &#125;)</code> returns the initial pair.</p>
  <pre><code>{`transition-duration: calc(var(--udx-motion-duration-medium2) * var(--udx-motion-scale));
transition-timing-function: var(--udx-motion-easing-standard);`}</code></pre>
  <MotionTokens tokens={['duration.short4', 'duration.medium2', 'duration.extraLong1', 'easing.linear', 'easing.standard', 'easing.standardDecelerate']} />

  <h3>Distances and staggers</h3>
  <p>The distances are <code>--udx-motion-distance-sm</code>, <code>-md</code> and <code>-lg</code> (8, 24 and 64 CSS pixels). The staggers are <code>--udx-motion-stagger-tight</code>, <code>-normal</code>, <code>-loose</code> and <code>-max</code> (20, 50, 100 and 400 milliseconds). These values are provisional, have low confidence and require visual calibration; they do not come from M3. <code>stagger-max</code> caps the relative start delay, not the end time of the last animation.</p>

  <h3>Document scale</h3>
  <p><code>--udx-motion-scale</code> is an inherited number, 1 by default, and 0 when <code>prefers-reduced-motion: reduce</code> is active or <code>data-motion-policy="reduce"</code> is set on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>. The value <code>auto</code>, an absent attribute and an unknown value follow the system preference. A motion multiplies the distances of its keyframes and its durations by the scale; a layout flight keeps its real geometry, and a gesture its own release. A scale of 0 is not a reduced render by itself: each verb applies its own (see <a href="/motion/accessibility">Reduced motion</a>).</p>
  <p>Search has its own outline transition: it works without the stylesheet, with a 200 ms duration and a fixed easing, and the stylesheet provides its tokens.</p>

  <h3>Experimental TypeScript tokens</h3>
  <pre><code>{`import { defineTokens, resolveSpring } from '@udixio/motion';

const tokens = defineTokens({ scheme: 'standard' }); // 'expressive' by default
tokens.spring.fast.spatial.dampingRatio; // the physical parameters
tokens.distance.md;                      // 24
tokens.stagger.normal;                   // 50`}</code></pre>
  <p>The spring of a reference is resolved for the backend that plays it: <code>resolveSpring('spring.fast.spatial', &#123; support &#125;)</code> picks the <code>linear()</code> curve where it is supported, and the fallback otherwise. The API shape may change while the shared motion model is designed.</p>

  <h3>Reading the tokens from a manifest</h3>
  <p>The machine-readable manifest lists every token name with its value, from <a href="/motion-manifest.json"><code>motion-manifest.json</code></a>, and the CSS variable each one is written as.</p>

  <h2>Reduced motion</h2>
  <p>The tokens do not disable animation. Apply the policy before you use a value (see <a href="/motion/accessibility">Reduced motion</a>). A number from <code>createSpring</code> reaches its target at once under reduced motion, which is why the balls above jump to their far end under the policy.</p>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The CSS spring pairs are static: the generated <code>linear()</code> curve's error bound applies to the analytical zero-velocity response over its own duration, not to the cubic-bezier fallback.</li>
    <li>Browser support of <code>linear()</code> is detected for the CSS syntax in that document, not for rendering fidelity in every backend.</li>
    <li>The distance and stagger tokens are provisional and have low confidence; they require visual calibration.</li>
  </ul>
</MotionPage>
